    "q-161233": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161233",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14223877/assets/6bd0ac15180df7a413691a1f1f15980b47e88c63e1d3af914175231c172ca97c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $3$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$",
      "worked_solution": "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(x(x-y)+y(y+x)+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =108\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
